/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   A token resolving to 69 in a leading comment opens a nested comment and
   swallows the rest of the file - see RMSTEST_56 and 57. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

  /* One compact patch, small relative to the placement radius on purpose. */
  create_terrain SNOW
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    number_of_clumps 1
    number_of_tiles 150
    clumping_factor 40
  }

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  /* THE ONLY OBJECT COMMAND IN THIS FILE. That is the whole design. */
  create_object OLIVE_TREE
  {
    number_of_objects 8
    number_of_groups 12
    group_placement_radius 6
    terrain_to_place_on SNOW
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_46a - grouping mode: NO MODE STATED. THE MEASUREMENT.
Feeds: preview-design Sec.6.6, objects.ts, Sec.15 item 17(c)

This file states no grouping mode. It is the one being measured; 46b and 46c
are its two reference points.

WHY THIS IS THREE FILES AND NOT THREE COMMANDS ON ONE MAP
RMSTEST_46 put all three modes on one map, distinguished only by which object
each command placed, and ran twice. THE TWO SITTINGS DISAGREED ABOUT WHICH
COMMAND SPILLED, and the disagreement cannot be resolved from the exports,
because a scenario records objects and positions and does NOT record which
command placed them. Attribution by object id looked free and was not.

The only change between the two sittings was a correction to a DIFFERENT
command's constant, which cannot alter an earlier command's behaviour - so the
attribution is what was wrong, not the engine. Both readings are withdrawn.

One command per file makes the reading unambiguous by construction. It costs two
extra generations.

THE QUESTION
Tight grouping checks the ANCHOR only and fills the rest of the group unchecked;
loose checks EVERY member. That is measured and is Sec.6.6. What has never been
tested is which applies when a command sets a group placement radius and states
no mode. The spec has said tight since an early revision.

It is not a remainder: 498 of the corpus's 964 grouped object commands declare
neither mode, so guessing wrong flips every constraint check for the majority of
grouped placements.

THE READING
Count how many objects sit OFF the patch. The three files share one patch size,
one radius and one group count, so the three off-patch fractions compare
directly.

  46b (tight) must spill and 46c (loose) must not. That pair is the calibration
  - if it fails, the instrument is broken and 46a cannot be read.

  46a matching 46b -> the default is TIGHT. The spec stands.
  46a matching 46c -> the default is LOOSE. The spec is wrong and half the
                      corpus's grouped placements change.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. THREE RUNS EACH, because the
off-patch fraction is a proportion.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW
  Then count objects on and off the patch. Absolute counts are not the reading -
  a group anchored near a patch edge spills more than one anchored centrally,
  which is why all three files share one patch and one radius.
*/
